## Summary

Replaces the external `@ai-sdk/devtools` integration (which required spawning a separate Hono HTTP server on port 4983 via `MUX_DEVTOOLS=1`) with a **first-party, in-process DevTools implementation** that records, persists, and live-streams all AI SDK interactions directly within Mux.

## Background

The existing `@ai-sdk/devtools` integration was limited: it required a separate process, only worked locally, and had no persistence. We wanted AI SDK DevTools-grade observability natively integrated into the Mux UI — including detailed token usage breakdowns, tool definitions, provider options, and raw HTTP request/response data.

## Implementation

### Backend
- **Data model** (`src/common/types/devtools.ts`): `DevToolsRun`, `DevToolsStep`, `DevToolsRunSummary` with rich token breakdowns (`DevToolsInputTokenBreakdown`/`DevToolsOutputTokenBreakdown` supporting cache read/write, reasoning tokens, and raw provider usage)
- **Service** (`src/node/services/devToolsService.ts`): `EventEmitter`-based service with in-memory `Map` + append-only JSONL persistence per workspace. Lazy-loads on first query, self-heals corrupted lines, and finalizes stale steps on startup
- **Middleware** (`src/node/services/devToolsMiddleware.ts`): `LanguageModelV3Middleware` intercepting `wrapGenerate`/`wrapStream` to capture prompts, tool definitions, provider options, token usage (with full breakdown), raw HTTP bodies, and SSE chunks
- **Header capture** (`src/node/services/devToolsHeaderCapture.ts`): Synthetic `x-mux-devtools-step-id` header correlation to capture outbound HTTP request/response headers without `AsyncLocalStorage`
- **Stream abort handling**: Direct `abortSignal` listener + stale step cleanup on middleware instantiation
- **oRPC routes**: `devtools.getRuns`, `devtools.getRunDetail`, `devtools.clear`, `devtools.subscribe` (live streaming via `createAsyncMessageQueue`)

### Frontend
- **Debug tab** in right sidebar with live subscription via oRPC generator
- **Step card** with AI SDK DevTools-inspired layout:
  - **Metadata bar**: Provider badge, model name, max tokens, tool choice, clickable pills for Available Tools / Provider Options / Usage
  - **Side-by-side Input/Output**: Input messages with role badges, Output with reasoning blocks (amber), tool call cards (violet), and text content
  - **Available Tools section**: Expandable tool list with names, descriptions, and JSON parameters
  - **Token Usage section**: Side-by-side Input/Output cards with cache read/write/no-cache and text/reasoning breakdowns, plus raw provider usage JSON
  - **Provider Options section**: Formatted JSON view
  - **Request/Response section**: Collapsible with AI SDK / Provider toggle, side-by-side JSON panels, and request/response headers
- **Settings toggle**: "API Debug Logs" in General settings
- **Dynamic tab injection**: Custom event `LLM_DEBUG_LOGS_CHANGED` toggles the Debug tab without window reload
- **Command palette**: Debug tab accessible via palette

### Cleanup
- Removed `@ai-sdk/devtools` npm dependency
- Removed `MUX_DEVTOOLS` environment variable gates
- Updated Nix flake hash

## Validation

- `make typecheck` ✅
- `make lint` ✅
- `make fmt-check` ✅ (our files)
- DevTools test suite: 31 tests across `devToolsService.test.ts`, `devToolsMiddleware.test.ts`, `devToolsHeaderCapture.test.ts` — all passing
- Manual testing via dev-server sandbox with live AI SDK calls

## Risks

- **Middleware performance**: The middleware intercepts every AI SDK call when enabled. Early-return when disabled ensures zero overhead. When enabled, the overhead is append-to-JSONL + in-memory map update per step — negligible compared to LLM latency.
- **JSONL file growth**: No automatic rotation yet. `clear()` truncates the file. Files are per-workspace and only written when the feature is toggled on.

